Yes, according to Wollstonecraft, boys and girls should be educated together in elementary day schools established by government. For children from five to nine years of age, these day schools should be "absolutely free and open to all classes" (Wollstonecraft, *A Vindication of the Rights of Woman*, Chapter XII, ¶43). 

Later, after the age of nine, boys and girls intended for domestic employments or mechanical trades should still be together in the morning, while older young people of superior abilities or fortune should also be taught together in another school (Wollstonecraft, *A Vindication of the Rights of Woman*, Chapter XII, ¶46–47). When asked if girls and boys should still be together, Wollstonecraft answers: "yes" (Wollstonecraft, *A Vindication of the Rights of Woman*, Chapter XII, ¶47).

*(Wollstonecraft, A Vindication of the Rights of Woman, Chapter XII, ¶43, 46–47)*

